well now to a fascinating and  significant story the media have all but  ignored  south africa is a diverse country but  the south african government would like  to make it much  less diverse an embattled minority of  farmers mostly afrikaans speaking  is being targeted in a wave of barbaric  and horrifying murders  but instead of protecting them the  government just passed a law  allowing it to seize their farms without  any compensation  based purely on their ethnicity and  distribute those farms to more favored  groups  thousands have already migrated out of  the country but they've struggled to  attack  attract any sympathy abroad for some  reason  ernst roots is deputy ceo of afra forum  it's a south africa civil rights group  he was just in the united states to meet  with a number of government officials  he also took a moment to talk to us  here's part of what he said  arch roost joins us now mr thanks for  coming on thank you very much  so what you described in this book  a disorganized but in some sense  intentional campaign  to crush a racial minority within your  country and the government  seems on board with it is that an  overstatement  well what what the book is about is it's  about government complicity in the  scourge of farm attacks and far murders  that we've seen in south africa  so we know in the last two decades  there's been according to government  statistics which we believe  we have reason to say that it's maybe  downplayed a little uh  there are about two there's been about  two farm attacks every day  and two farm murders every week uh in  south africa  and uh in the book i highlight actually  ten different reasons uh why we believe  the south african government is  complicit  and it stretches from reasons including  the fact that as these attacks have  worsened for example in 2007  there was a 25 increase in these attacks  and then the south african government  responded saying we're not going to  release any more statistics about this  then also which is very concerning is  the the  uh the hate speech in south africa so we  have that's the title of the book as  well kill the buddha  which is a very popular song which is  still being sung in south africa it was  sung just the other day  so it's sung at political rallies and of  course referring to a particular  cultural  group in south africa right we have  songs like shu tabu which is a different  song  so there are variety of ways in which we  but i thought that hate speech  was now a crime punishable by prison  time in south africa or any of the  people who say this  arrested well there's a a bill on the  table to have hate speech declared a  crime  uh punishable but by prison time but  if you look at the motivation around  this it's clear  very clear that i think those in  government who are pushing this  don't regard this as hate speech as a  matter of fact they've been arguing that  it's not hate speech in court so they  would argue that they have a right to  sing about  killing killing farmers and then they  would say it's an expression of freedom  of speech  so now the government has announced  that it plans to pursue a policy of  taking land away from people on the  basis of their skin color do you think  they'll do that  well that's what that's what was adopted  in in our parliament in february a  motion was adopted that the  property rights clause in the south  african constitution has to be changed  to allow for expropriation of private  property without compensation  so the state president sir ramaphosa  said that this would turn south africa  into the ultimate paradise and the  garden of eden  and deputy president david mabusa even  went further saying that  basically threatening white farmers that  if they do not  voluntarily hand over their land to  black people then there would be a  violent takeover  so the situation is very dire in south  africa but the good news is that that  there's still a process that has to be  followed and that's why we are in the  united states there's a sense that the  people being murdered  deserve it because of what their  ancestors did  and the international community is  similarly silent  have you met with any government  officials here in washington about  what's happening in your country well  that's why we are here  we are about to meet with some  government officials and it's very  unfortunate the situation  uh that you've just mentioned so there's  especially in south africa there's a  hierarchy of recognition  so some people when there are the  victims it's all over the news and you  hear about it frequently and other  people  as especially i think especially farmers  in south africa white farmers in  particular  whether they would be tortured to death  and it would receive very little news  coverage and that's unfortunately the  situation abroad as well  which is why we are very grateful to be  having this so a government official in  australia said publicly maybe we ought  to allow asylum to people being  persecuted for their skin color in your  country  and he was basically laughed at yeah and  then attacked  by your government for saying that yes  he was attacked and um they then said  that he shouldn't say that because he  was  he um afriforum was providing them with  false information while the information  that we are quoting is government  statistics  um so that is the situation uh whoever  speaks out against this we  just for being in the united states  we've been accused of treason we've had  people threatening us  saying they'll wait for us at the  airport when we come back and they'll  attack us and so forth  we had the state president today in  parliament calling us calling on us to  go back to south africa  um so whenever you speak out against  this there's a very serious backlash  this is not what nelson mandela wanted  no it's the exact opposite than what he  wanted he  he publicly he spoke about a free  country in which people were not judged  by the color of their skin  and he even wanted uh pharma text to be  a priority crime he said that when he  was president and when his um  presidency was over they deprioritized  it so you just watched something like  this happen in a neighboring  country zimbabwe which went from being  one the richest in africa to the poorest  in the world  because of farm uh attacks and because  of the theft of private property  are the is the anc the ruling party in  south africa  aware of that lesson yeah well they are  but you know that saying real communism  has never been tried that's what they  keep saying so  it failed in zimbabwe it failed in cuba  it failed in venezuela it failed in  russia it failed in china in all of  those countries  and they they're basically saying that  we're going to try the same thing  but this time it's going to work so last  question it's considered basically  immoral in the united states to talk to  you or to  hear stories like this for reasons that  nobody ever bothers really to explain  but it's just understood  if you could speak to our media or the  people who are dismissing  your claims as false what would you say  we want them to call on the south  african government to say listen you are  calling on us to invest in in your  country but you want to expropriate  those investments uh number two we are  we are speaking with  the us government and asking them to put  pressure on the south african government  which  could be done in a variety of ways and  number three members of the public and  members of the media the  best thing that you can do to help us is  to talk about this to talk about it on  public platforms and  in that way to continue to put pressure  on the south african government not to  continue with this policy just to tell  the truth i agree mr  mr thank you thank you very much thank  you for being

